cartridge "BRICKBUSTERS/P.SEEBURGER/1986"

' BRICKBUSTERS
' A ColecoVision game based on "Brickbusters" by Paul Seeburger, Ahoy!
' March 1986 (Commodore 64 machine language at $C000, started with
' SYS 49152). Made for Amy Studio by Claude (an AI made by Anthropic),
' 2026, in Amy by Amy Bienvenu.
'
' Not a line-by-line port: the rules are the original's (read from its
' machine code, orig/brickbusters.dis), the game is built for the
' ColecoVision: bevelled bricks in the original's thirteen colours, a
' riveted steel frame, a smooth sprite ball and paddle, a sound for every
' event, a title tune.
'
' The game: knock every brick out of the wall with the ball. Each brick is
' worth a point. Miss the ball and it is lost; the last one ends that
' player's game. Clear the wall and the next player takes a turn on a fresh
' wall; when every player has had a turn the level goes up: the next of
' the four walls, a faster ball, and one more ball for everybody.
'
' From the original (orig/): the four options (ball speed 0-4, paddle
' ("board") speed 0-9, paddle size small or large, 1 to 4 players, taking
' turns); the four walls and their row colours (8, 10, 13 and 13 lines with
' gaps: 6, 8, 9 and 13 rows of bricks), cycling with the level; three balls
' a player, one more for each player at every new level; a point a brick,
' seven-digit scores and a high score kept between games; a player's turn
' lasts until the wall is clear or the last ball is lost, a fresh wall for
' every turn; the serve: the ball starts low at a random place and leaves
' upward with one of sixteen directions drawn at random (the C64's table:
' sideways speed -4..4 against an upward speed of 2, or +-1 against 2, 3 or
' 4); a brick or the paddle turns the ball's vertical direction, walls turn
' it; on the paddle the stick adds "English": moving left sends the ball
' left one step faster, moving right sends it right, standing still keeps
' its angle; PLAYER n before each turn, GAME OVER FOR PLAYER n; pause.
'
' Liberties: the field is 14 bricks wide (the C64's 20 did not fit in
' columns 2-29) and scaled to 224 x 140 pixels; speeds are in 1/16 pixel
' a frame: ball speed 0-4 gives 5-9 sixteenths per step of the C64's
' direction table (a vertical step of 2-4), and the ball gains one more
' every second level up to four more (the C64's delay loop shrank by a
' unit a round and its fastest settings ran far too fast for a person);
' paddle speed 0-9 is 0.75-6.4 pixels a frame (the C64 moved 1-10 pixels a
' 60th of a second); the paddles are 16 and 32 pixels wide (18 and 36 on a
' 320-pixel C64 field), the default speed is 5; the paddle's English stops
' at a sideways speed of 5 (the C64's grew without limit); the paddle only
' bounces a ball that is falling; collisions are tests on the ball's
' corners and the paddle's box (the C64 read its collision registers);
' the right button pauses (the C64 used SHIFT), the menu replaces the typed
' answers, the keyboard choice and RUN/STOP are gone; the ball waits on the
' paddle's row for a button (the C64 waited for the fire button too);
' sounds for bricks, walls, paddle, serve, a cleared wall; the end: GAME
' OVER, then the title (the C64 went back to its questions).
'
' Controls: controller 1 (or 2): the stick moves the paddle left and
' right, a button serves, the right button pauses. On the title, up and
' down choose a line, left and right change it, a button starts.
'
' House rules: text in columns 2-29, rows 1-22; tiles from code 128; the
' frame loop is protected from the NMI; screens drawn with the NMI off
' after a wait, display off. Music first in the sound table, effects after;
' the title tune is played once. `set sprite count` is always given. Own
' xorshift random numbers.

